it was one of the defining moments in  world war ii a mission that turned the  tide of history  the doo little raiders were the first  pilots to successfully strike japan  following the japanese attack on pearl  harbor the raid has been well documented  but the story of what happened after has  not been told in full  until now both the raid and the little  known history that followed are covered  in michelle  paradis new book last mission to tokyo  it was seen as miraculous you know the  united states had been losing the war  up to that point and the doolittle raid  was the first  moment when americans really thought not  only  should we win this war we can win this  war just  four months earlier the japanese attack  on pearl harbor  pulled america into world war ii i asked  that the congress declare a state  of war has existed between the united  states  and the japanese empire but it also told  us  the u.s did not at the time have the  technical prowess to win  because american fighter planes could  not reach japan  this is really a book in two parts yeah  michelle  parody is the author of the new book  last mission to tokyo  a combination epic war story and high  stakes courtroom drama  we met him at the american air power  museum in farmingdale new york  april 1942 that's a dark period of world  war ii  uh we'd lost the philippines the baton  death march results in ten thousand  american soldiers dying  tens of thousands more being interned by  the japanese the british lose shanghai  singapore all of their major possessions  americans did not have a way to win  in the pacific until a pioneering pilot  named  jimmy doolittle was put in charge of a  top secret raid that many considered  a suicide mission not until they are  miles at sea to these men  know definitely the mission for which  they have volunteered  his charge was to get 16 b-25  bombers from the deck of the u.s  aircraft carrier hornet  into the heart of japan to strike tokyo  so they basically rip out everything  inside the plane that doesn't  require it to get into the air they take  out all the guns except for that front  gun  uh they take out all the safety  equipment  and they fill it with gasoline they  basically put these giant gas tanks  throughout the plane so they could have  these flying gas cans  with four bombs in them get their way  over tokyo and onto japan  originally planning to fly 400 miles on  the day of the mission  the hornet spotted an enemy patrol boat  fearing they'd be discovered the u.s  moved up the attack by 10 hours  and they launched from 650 miles off the  coast of japan  it's airborne the first army bomber ever  to be launched from a carrier  is safely in the air with no fuel for  the ride back  the raiders were eventually forced to  parachute out and let their planes crash  land but their bombs did reach their  targets  later in china the flyers who bailed out  safely  are decorated by madam chiang kai-shek  the doolittle raid  pushed japan back on its heels it turned  it from a country that thought it was  going to win the more  and was making the kind of decisions a  winner makes to win  and moved it to an entirely defensive  strategy that ultimately made  the it completely literally changed the  tide  following the raid japan made a series  of strategically  disastrous decisions culminating in the  battle of midway  a stunning american victory but as all  that happened  the fate of some of the doolittle  raiders remained in question  80 men were on the mission 77 survived  but  eight were captured while in japanese  prisons  they were accused of murdering civilians  tortured  and three of the doolittle raiders were  executed following a short  show trial a fourth died of starvation  and malnutrition  the survivors were left with lifelong  physical and mental scars  they tortured americans they murdered  americans they starved them to death and  imprisoned them in solitary confinement  driving them half mad  parade who's also a lawyer for the  defense department and has defended  guantanamo detainees  says this book is the first in-depth  recounting of the trial of the japanese  soldiers responsible  which took place in shanghai after the  war in 1946  the thing that really grabbed me and  made me want to invest in this story  when i first started researching it was  that we gave the japanese a fair trial  the book includes new details about the  prosecution's investigation  deep into the fog of war to find the  perpetrators of a crime against  america's greatest heroes  and the stories of those tasked with  defending the accused  a role that put them in the crosshairs  of public opinion while trying to uphold  american ideals that includes the lead  defense lawyer colonel edmund bodeen  a pilot himself here he's representing  someone who is hated  by everyone in the country who everyone  assumes is just going to be executed so  they assume he has an impossible mission  too  and nevertheless he commits to doing it  the right way there was pressure to not  do it that way  absolutely there was a lot of pressure  to just execute people  um and one of the forgotten pieces of  the post-war history was the real debate  should we have these  war crimes trials at all the four  japanese men  on trial were ultimately given between  five and nine years  hard labor parody says shanghai set the  stage for better known trials that came  after  the tokyo and nuremberg war crimes  tribunals  which held high-ranking officials of the  japanese empire  and nazi germany accountable for their  actions  and he says it became the precedent for  laws that still govern  modern warfare the doolittle raid goes  from being an important military victory  to an important victory for american  values and ultimately succeeded in  not just making the the standard for  american allies around the world but  succeeded in making  international law the basic expectations  about human rights about fair trials  about the prohibition on torture  did not envy michelle's job going  through this massive amount of material  but he did a really nice job stringing  it  together it's always nice to hear about  a piece of history that you might not be  familiar with yeah and also just the  impact that he was just talking about at  the end there  that it's that lasting impact sometimes  doing the right thing  at the hardest time becomes the most  important thing we still try to live by  today
 

